## CRITICAL RULE: Task Plan Alignment and Task Plan Sync

**At session start, verify task queue alignment before acting.** Then update task_plan.md as part of every non-Quick task completion.

- Read `Projects/[Name]/task_plan.md` if it exists; skip this rule for projects without one.
- Identify the top-of-queue `[ ]` item; verify it still matches STATE.md `## Next`.
- If mismatch, flag to user before acting.
- After a non-Quick task ships (QA PASS), update its `task_plan.md` entry: mark `[x]`, append a 1-3 line result summary (what shipped, where the artifact lives, QA outcome).
- Do this BEFORE invoking the end-of-task PM checkpoint (not the task-start classifier PM trigger); task_plan.md must reflect current reality when PM reads it.
- If the task surfaced follow-up work (new tickets), append them to task_plan.md in the same edit.
- The update is part of definition-of-done; a task is not complete until task_plan.md is synced. On QA FAIL, add a note to the entry describing the failure rather than marking [x]; the entry stays open until QA PASSes.
- **Do not claim a state-file save before the tool confirms it (2026-07-15, F-4).** Do not write "task_plan.md updated" or "STATE.md saved" until the Write or Edit tool call has returned without error. The save and the report are separate events.

## CRITICAL RULE: Quality Verification (Three-Tier QA)

QA is Popperian falsification — proves absence of *found* bugs.

- **Tier 1** (`process-qa`): Per-task. Verify claims empirically. QA REPORT with PASS/FAIL. Reports bugs, doesn't fix.
- **Tier 2** (`process-pentest`): Per-increment. Adversarial execution. PENTEST REPORT + Untested Surface.
- **Tier 3** (promptfoo): Per-milestone. Human-triggered YAML assertion suites.

Tier N prerequisite for Tier N+1. **Untested Surface mandatory** on every report.

**The QA and PM blocks are not pasted into chat (2026-09-11, owner ruling).** Wiktor, verbatim: "yeah i mean the QA and PM notes, I dont want to see them". The full QA SCOPE and QA REPORT go to the project's `work/qa-log.md`; the reply carries three lines (`QA SCOPE:` pointer, `QA REPORT:`, `PASS: n / N | FAIL: ... | Untested: ...`). Nothing is weakened: all four hooks that read these strings were verified still firing on the compact form. The colon after `QA REPORT` and the line-initial `PASS: n / N` are load-bearing. PM blocks disappear by simply not invoking `/pm`, whose mandate is already suspended. Format contract: `.claude/skills/process-qa/SKILL.md` section "Compact relay".

**Declarative-first for Build (2026-06-02):** When a Build task has a checkable outcome, write the check *before* the implementation — the failing test/assertion is the spec. "Make X work" becomes "here is the test X must pass." Reduces the implement-then-rationalize bias the abandoned-TDD pattern documents.

**Per-step CHECK discipline (ratified 2026-08-01, [[2026-08-01-iteration-decomposition-proposal]]):** every TaskCreate step declares a one-line `CHECK:` clause naming its executable check (taxonomy and full rule in task-classifier Step 6 rule 1a); a step whose check ran and failed is not complete. Quick tasks and one-sentence-diff imperatives are exempt.

**Citation close-out, dark-zone discipline (2026-07-15, F-8).** After all agents return and before writing the final response, list each agent dispatch by agent name and cite at least one specific finding from its output (a path, a quote, or a count). A response that dispatched one or more agents and contains zero citations from their outputs is a dark-zone event: it signals the orchestrator may be sourcing from training context rather than sub-agent findings, and it will be flagged. This matters most on high-effort and extra-high-effort turns, where citation discipline is the guard against the fabrication substrate.

## CRITICAL RULE: Delegation

**NEVER produce analysis, evaluation, or documents inline when a specialist exists.** Delegate first. Parallel when multiple apply.

**Delegate if ANY apply:** produces document/spec/plan/report/prompt | evaluates quality against rubric | reads 3+ items detecting patterns | involves LLM prompts | compares outputs | crosses specialist domain.

**Inline ONLY for:** single-field edits, one-sentence answers, zero-judgment file moves.

**Memory-folder ownership (2026-08-18, advisory wall).** The memory folder (`.claude/projects/<id>/memory/`) is main-session-owned. Subagents do not write it; they report memory-worthy findings back to the orchestrator, which decides what persists. Enforcement is advisory: `memory-context-guard.py` (PreToolUse) warns on subagent-context writes and logs every memory-folder write to `aggregates/memory-context-warnings.jsonl`; a persistence nudge (`memory-nudge.py`) reminds the main session past 10 quiet turns, and "nothing to save" is a valid outcome. The deny tier (`aggregate-write-guard.py`, three singleton files) is unchanged. Origin: [[2026-08-17-hermes-p2-memory-governance-plan]].

**Model cost:** Always `model: "sonnet"` for Explore, general-purpose, Plan. Opus only when needed. Each spawn loads ~60K context.

### Blind Analysis Rule

Delegation messages for evaluation contain ONLY what to examine and criteria. No hypotheses, prior conclusions, proposed fixes, or "right answer" framing.

**Test:** "Does my message contain a proposed cause or conclusion?" If yes, rewrite. Applies to all dispatches except blueprint-mode (needs spec), implementation-plan (needs requirements), content-marketer (needs brief), adversarial-reviewer (needs what to challenge).

